//! An HTTP client, in the browser: requests go through the browser's
//! `fetch`, once the policy allows `Net(host:port)`.
//!
//! Compared with other builds: a response from another site can be read
//! only if the site allows it (CORS), and only the headers it exposes are
//! visible; the browser sets some request headers itself (`host`,
//! `cookie`, `user-agent`, ...), and sends no cookies. Redirects are not
//! followed: the browser does not say where they lead, so the policy could
//! not decide on them; a redirect fails with kind `"http"`. Host names are
//! decided as written (the browser resolves them; the addresses are not
//! checked). There is no server. Response bodies larger than the
//! `max_http_body` limit fail with kind `"limit"`.

import json
